---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { REPO } from '../../lib/site';
---
<Layout
  title="Howdah troubleshooting & FAQ"
  description="Common Howdah questions and fixes: loaded in CLI but not FPM, detect vs block, upgrading, uninstalling, thread safety, and performance."
  path="/docs/troubleshooting/"
>
  <div class="pagehead">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/docs/">Docs</a> / <span aria-current="page">Troubleshooting</span></p>
      <h1>Troubleshooting &amp; FAQ</h1>
      <p class="lede">The questions that come up most, and how to settle them.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>

      <h3>It loads on the command line but not in FPM / Apache</h3>
      <p>PHP has a separate configuration per SAPI. <code>php --ri howdah</code> working on the CLI does not mean the web server loaded it. Enable it for the running SAPI and reload that service:</p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code><span class="c"># Debian/Ubuntu (Surý)</span>
sudo phpenmod -v 8.1 howdah
sudo systemctl reload php8.1-fpm       <span class="c"># or: systemctl reload apache2</span>

<span class="c"># then confirm against the SAPI's own config</span>
php-fpm8.1 -i | grep -i howdah          <span class="c"># or check a phpinfo() page</span></code></pre>
      <p>On Apache <code>mod_php</code>, the module is loaded by the web server process, so reload Apache, not FPM.</p>

      <h3>The self-test is flagged but an over-limit request is not blocked</h3>
      <p>PHP's own <code>max_input_vars</code> (default 1000) can stop parsing before Howdah's higher <code>max_input_vars</code> (2000) is reached — PHP is protecting itself first, which is fine. For the multipart limits, confirm the request really crosses the limit, and that the rule is not set to <code>detect</code>/<code>off</code> in your TOML.</p>

      <h3>A request logged an event but still returned HTTP 200</h3>
      <p>Expected for the input-shape and request-header rules on some SAPIs: the offending variable is dropped and logged (the real mitigation), but the 403 is best-effort and may not land before the script has already produced output. The multipart limits, by contrast, block before the script runs. See <a href="/docs/configuration/">Enforcement timing</a>.</p>

      <h3>Nothing is being blocked at all</h3>
      <p>Check the global mode (<code>php --ri howdah</code> shows it). In <code>detect</code> mode only the Critical rules block; Standard rules log. Switch to <code>block</code> once the logs look clean. Confirm the log path is writable (the <code>Log destination</code> row of <code>php --ri howdah</code> shows where lines go): otherwise they fall back to stderr, which php-fpm discards unless <code>catch_workers_output = yes</code>. <code>[log] destination = "syslog"</code> avoids file permissions altogether.</p>

      <h3>My config has a typo: what happens?</h3>
      <p>PHP keeps serving, and Howdah falls back to <strong>block</strong> mode with its default limits, not to your settings. That can refuse requests you meant only to log. It is not silent: PHP's error log (or stderr on the CLI) gets one line at startup, <code>Howdah: config file '…' is invalid, running in block mode instead: at line 3, column 1: unknown field …</code>, and <code>php --ri howdah</code> shows <code>Config status =&gt; INVALID (fell back to block): …</code>. Fix the line it names, validate with the <a href="/config/">config editor</a>, and reload PHP. If you would rather a typo disable Howdah than block, add <code>on_invalid_config = "off"</code> as a top-level line; see <a href="/docs/configuration/">Configuration</a>.</p>

      <h3>Which php.ini settings make my site easier to attack?</h3>
      <p><code>php --ri howdah</code> ends with a "Risky ini settings" table listing only the flagged ones (<code>none flagged</code> if there are none), and <code>howdah-check ini</code> prints the same checks with the current value, a one-line reason and what to set instead. Both are read-only and change nothing. They flag <code>register_argc_argv</code> (a precondition for Craft CMS CVE-2024-56145 and Laravel CVE-2024-52301), <code>allow_url_include</code>, <code>session.upload_progress.enabled</code>, a world-readable or docroot <code>session.save_path</code>, <code>display_errors</code>, <code>expose_php</code>, <code>enable_dl</code>, <code>cgi.fix_pathinfo</code> under CGI/FastCGI, and any of <code>exec</code>, <code>system</code>, <code>passthru</code>, <code>shell_exec</code>, <code>popen</code>, <code>proc_open</code> missing from <code>disable_functions</code>. This is a list of known preconditions, not a security audit: a clean report does not mean the site is safe, and a flagged setting is not an exploit. The docroot test is a path-name guess. <code>howdah-check</code> reads the CLI binary's settings, which can differ from php-fpm's or Apache's.</p>

      <h2>Upgrading</h2>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code><span class="c"># apt</span>
sudo apt-get update &amp;&amp; sudo apt-get install --only-upgrade php8.1-howdah
sudo systemctl reload php8.1-fpm
<span class="c"># yum / dnf</span>
sudo yum update php81-howdah
<span class="c"># cPanel, Plesk, CloudLinux, distribution EL PHP: the same, with that package's name</span>
sudo dnf update ea-php81-php-howdah    <span class="c"># or plesk-php81-howdah, alt-php81-howdah, php-howdah</span>
<span class="c"># tarball: verify + replace howdah.so for your PHP minor, then reload</span></code></pre>
      <p class="legend">Always verify the signature of a new tarball before replacing (see the <a href="/install/">install picker</a>). Your TOML and ini are not touched by an upgrade.</p>

      <h2>Uninstalling</h2>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code><span class="c"># apt: stop loading but keep the package, or remove entirely</span>
sudo phpdismod -v 8.1 howdah &amp;&amp; sudo systemctl reload php8.1-fpm
sudo apt-get purge php8.1-howdah
<span class="c"># yum</span>
sudo yum remove php81-howdah
<span class="c"># cPanel, Plesk, CloudLinux, distribution EL PHP: remove that package</span>
sudo dnf remove ea-php81-php-howdah    <span class="c"># or plesk-php81-howdah, alt-php81-howdah, php-howdah</span>
<span class="c"># tarball: remove the extension=howdah.so line (or the conf.d file) and reload</span></code></pre>
      <p class="legend">Removal never breaks PHP: the package maintainer scripts only enable/disable the module and never fail the transaction.</p>

      <h2>FAQ</h2>
      <dl class="faq">
        <dt>Does Howdah replace patching or upgrading PHP?</dt>
        <dd>No. Upgrade PHP when you can. Howdah is for when you cannot, or until you can — especially on EOL PHP that will never get the official fix.</dd>

        <dt>Will it break my site?</dt>
        <dd>It is designed to fail open: the intent is that a bug in Howdah never crashes or wedges PHP. Start in <code>detect</code> mode, watch the logs, then move to <code>block</code>. The false-positive corpus (ordinary galleries, settings forms, nested arrays) passes with zero blocks at the default limits.</dd>

        <dt>Does it phone home or need a cloud service?</dt>
        <dd>No. Howdah runs entirely inside your PHP. It makes no outbound connections and needs no account or external service.</dd>

        <dt>Which PHP builds are supported?</dt>
        <dd>Non-thread-safe (NTS) PHP 5.4 through 8.5 on Linux x86_64 and aarch64, one build per minor version. ZTS builds are not supported. Howdah 1.5 ships glibc and musl builds, and public Docker images (Debian PHP 5.6&ndash;8.5; <code>-alpine</code> 7.2&ndash;8.5, amd64 only); see the <a href="/install/">install guide</a>. See <a href="/coverage/">coverage</a> for what has been tested.</dd>

        <dt>How do I silence a known false positive?</dt>
        <dd>Add a scoped <code>[[exceptions]]</code> entry (by script, parameter, URI prefix or client CIDR) with a <code>reason</code>, rather than turning the rule off. See <a href="/docs/configuration/">configuration</a>. Critical Layer 1 limits take no exception.</dd>

        <dt>What is the performance cost?</dt>
        <dd>Howdah counts parts and variables as PHP already parses them; there is no second parse of the body. It adds bounded bookkeeping per request and does not buffer the whole body itself. Measured on one machine, a request with no body took a few microseconds longer with Howdah loaded, and Layer 2 adds time in proportion to the form values it scans (tens of microseconds for a 1&nbsp;KB form). The numbers, the method and the OPcache JIT result are on the <a href="/docs/performance/">performance page</a>.</dd>

        <dt>How do I report a security issue?</dt>
        <dd>Privately, through GitHub's vulnerability reporting — see the <a href={`${REPO}/security/policy`}>security policy</a>. Please do not open a public issue for a suspected vulnerability.</dd>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .faq { margin: 1rem 0 0; }
  .faq dt { font-weight: 600; font-family: var(--display); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 1.5rem; }
  .faq dd { margin: .4rem 0 0; max-width: 46rem; }
</style>
